17,0.102,0.032,0.011,0.069,0.062,0.056,0.04,0.029,0.041,0.033,0.137,0.053,0.068,0.06,0.107,0.123,0.047,0.057,0.038,0.018,0.068,0.101,0.135,0.258,0.103,0.254,0.224,0.247,0.082,0.087,0.081,0.032,0.04,0.051,0.105,0.069,0.188,0.078,0.05,0.073,0.17,0.146,0.13,0.047,0.044,0.113,0.134,0.088,0.006,0.063,0.028,0.049,0.009,0.009,0.087,0.133,0.087,0.082,0.071,0.209,0.226,0.143,0.092,0.091,0.087,0.082,0.109,0.096,0.153,0.069,0.063,0.111,0.117,0.127,0.135,0.03,0.006,0.008,0.065,0.075,0.083,0.042,0.042,0.071,0.09,0.173,0.098,0.165,0.095,0.076,0.134,0.045,0.039,0.01,0.01,0.028,0.005,0.023,0.121,0.039,0.015,0.061,0.052,0.11,0.083,0.043,0.115,0.034,0.055,0.119,0.074,0.06,0.059,0.057,0.105,0.077,0.037,0.026,0.002,0.014,0.043,0.146,0.032,0.064,0.057,0.109,0.1,0.035,0.006,0.011,0.003,0,0.029,0.118,0.058,0.048,0.022,0.054,0.045,0.086,0.179,0.032,0.039,0.106,0.049,0.046,0.03,0.008,0.025,0.15,0.195,0.008,0.055,0.048,0.009,0.067,0.043,0.084,0.034,0.056,0.062,0.039,0.039,0.085,0.069,0.045,0.005,0.057,0.073,0.026,0.001,0.167,0.087,0.011]}},"alerts":[{"event":"Hydrologic Outlook","headline":"Hydrologic Outlook issued September 27 at 1:01PM MDT by NWS Grand Junction CO","description":"ESFGJT\n\nStrong southwesterly winds ahead of a closed low centered over\nCalifornia will pull tropical moisture from Tropical Storm Odalys and\nHurricane Polo towards eastern Utah and western Colorado, with\natmospheric moisture peaking in our area on Monday. The northerly\nextent of the expected excessive rainfall poses a threat to our\nsouthern San Juan river basins. Current forecasts suggest 1 to 4\ninches of rain Monday morning through early Wednesday. At face value,\nrivers and tributaries will see a rise due to the forecast rainfall.\nHowever, localized heavier amounts could result in flooding across\nthe southern San Juan foothills and communities further downstream.\n\nRivers have responded rapidly to recent rainfall due to saturated\nsoils and increased runoff potential. Thus, prolonged periods of\nadditional rainfall over these regions would trigger a similar\nresponse. The current precipitation forecast suggests larger peaks in\nseveral creeks and rivers than what has been recently observed.\nHowever, high end probabilistic rainfall amounts (i.e. above 90th\npercentile) may lead to rivers exceeding action stage to minor\nflooding. Most probable basins to see significant rises include; the\nRio Blanco, San Juan, Piedra, Los Pinos/Vallecito Creek, and the\nAnimas River Basins.\n\nConvective storms and periods of heavy rainfall fed from said\ntropical moisture could result in localized flooding impacts across\nthe CWA, however, confidence decreases rapidly north of the San\nJuans. Confidence has increased enough for a Flood Watch to be issued\nfor southern portions of the forecast area valid from 6am Monday\nthrough 6am Wednesday. Continue to monitor the latest forecasts\nespecially for those who reside and/or plan to recreate in the Watch\nareas.","ends":"2026-09-28T15:15:00-06:00","severity":"Unknown","areaDesc":"Archuleta, CO; Dolores, CO; Gunnison, CO; Hinsdale, CO; La Plata, CO; Mesa, CO; Montezuma, CO; Montrose, CO; Ouray, CO; San Juan, CO; San Miguel, CO; Grand, UT; San Juan, UT","id":"urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.7d95a2138492d35262c0408a70acac4faa29c1f7.001.1","url":"https://api.weather.gov/alerts/urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.7d95a2138492d35262c0408a70acac4faa29c1f7.001.1"},{"event":"Flood Watch","headline":"Flood Watch issued September 27 at 10:27AM MDT until September 30 at 6:00AM MDT by NWS Grand Junction CO","description":"* WHAT...Flooding caused by excessive rainfall continues to be\npossible.\n\n* WHERE...Portions of Colorado, including the following areas, Animas\nRiver Basin, Four Corners/Upper Dolores River, Northwest San Juan\nMountains, Paradox Valley/Lower Dolores River, San Juan River\nBasin, Southwest San Juan Mountains and Uncompahgre Plateau and\nDallas Divide and southeast Utah, including the following areas,\nCanyonlands/Natural Bridges, La Sal and Abajo Mountains and\nSoutheast Utah.\n\n* WHEN...From Monday morning through late Tuesday night.\n\n* IMPACTS...Flooding will be possible over burn scars, at low-water\ncrossings, and along creeks, streams, washes, arroyos and other\nflood-prone locations. Creeks and streams may rise out of their\nbanks. Unpaved roads could become muddy and impassable.\n\n* ADDITIONAL DETAILS...\n- Periods of heavy rainfall, associated with remnant moisture from\nHurricane Polo, are expected to produce numerous showers and\nembedded thunderstorms. Heavy rain falling on saturated soils and\nburn scars could lead to localized flash flooding and rises on\nlocal rivers.\n- http://www.weather.gov/safety/flood","ends":"2026-09-30T06:00:00-06:00","severity":"Severe","areaDesc":"Uncompahgre Plateau/Dallas Divide; Northwestern San Juan Mountains; Southwest San Juan Mountains; Paradox Valley/Lower Dolores River; Four Corners/Upper Dolores River; Animas River Basin; San Juan River Basin; Southeast Utah; La Sal and Abajo Mountains; Canyonlands/Natural Bridges","id":"urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.4e0b211dcd7bab58026a9cb01526038e869174d9.001.1","url":"https://api.weather.gov/alerts/urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.4e0b211dcd7bab58026a9cb01526038e869174d9.001.1"}],"afd":{"office":"GJT","issued":"2026-09-27T18:55:00+00:00","snippet":"000 FXUS65 KGJT 271855 AFDGJT Area Forecast Discussion National Weather Service Grand Junction CO 1255 PM MDT Sun Sep 27 2026 .KEY MESSAGES... - Warmer and drier conditions continue today. - Increased moisture will arrive by Monday, bringing widespread moderate to heavy precipitation through midweek. - Chances of prolonged rainfall increases flooding concerns in southeast Utah and southwest Colora","url":"https://forecast.weather.gov/product.php?site=GJT&issuedby=GJT&product=AFD&format=CI"},"hwo":null,"fwf":{"office":"GJT","issued":"2026-09-27T07:37:00+00:00","snippet":".DISCUSSION... Once again, a few showers, maybe a quick storm, will be possible over higher terrain this afternoon and evening. Elsewhere, quiet conditions are expected. Widespread precipitation returns Monday through Wednesday asremnant moisture from tropical systems moves in, especially down south. Clouds and precip will keep critical fire weather conditions at a minimum. ...GUSTY ERRATIC WINDS ","url":"https://forecast.weather.gov/product.php?site=GJT&issuedby=GJT&product=FWF&format=CI"},"coagmet":{"station_id":"orm02","station_name":"CSU Orchard Mesa Research Station","distance_km":7.5,"soil_temp_5cm_f":70.11,"soil_temp_15cm_f":66.11,"soil_moisture_5cm":null,"soil_moisture_15cm":null,"precip_in":null,"eto_in":null,"vapor_pressure":null,"solar_radiation":715.33,"wind_speed_mph":3.51,"air_temp_f":78.03,"relative_humidity":0.388,"url":"https://coagmet.colostate.edu/station/orm02"},"aviation":{"icao":"KGJT","distance_km":0,"raw_metar":"METAR KGJT 271853Z 13009KT 10SM BKN110 25/07 A3012 RMK AO2 SLP137 T02500067","temp_f":77,"wind_dir":130,"wind_kt":9,"gust_kt":null,"visibility":"10+","cover":"BKN","flight_category":"VFR","altimeter":1020.1,"observed":1790535180,"raw_taf":"TAF KGJT 271729Z 2718/2818 13008KT P6SM FEW130 SCT200 FM272000 VRB03KT P6SM SCT110 BKN130 FM280500 12007KT P6SM SCT100 BKN120 FM280900 11011KT P6SM SCT070 BKN100 FM281500 14011KT 6SM -SHRA BKN060 OVC080","url":"https://aviationweather.gov/data/metar/?ids=KGJT&hours=0"},"airgradient":null,"airnow":{"aqi":32,"category":"Good","parameter":"O3","reporting_area":"Grand Junction","observed":"2026-09-27 12","by_parameter":{"O3":{"aqi":32,"category":"Good","reporting_area":"Grand Junction","observed":"2026-09-27 12"}},"url":"https://www.airnow.gov/","distance_km":8.3},"openmeteo_aq":{"pm25":3.7,"pm10":10.7,"co":106,"no2":0.1,"so2":0.3,"o3":100,"european_aqi":40,"us_aqi":26,"time":"2026-09-27T13:00"},"usgs":{"station_id":"09106485","station_name":"COLORADO R. ABV GUNNISON R. AT GRAND JUNCTION, CO","distance_km":1.3,"discharge_cfs":460,"gauge_height_ft":3.12,"water_temp_f":66,"observed":"2026-09-27T12:45:00.000-06:00","url":